From 9bcbfef0e0f59b0d45bdc1a8ba95bf2ca6a54c32 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Flores Digest Automation <frank@floresinsuranceagency.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 17:29:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] Hold the email body near 50 KB -- the real ceiling is the
 send path's, not Gmail's

Probed the live pipeline rather than guessing:

     6 KB body  -> arrives, <style> byte-for-byte intact
    52 KB body  -> arrives, 1,118 correct "=3D" escapes, style intact
    84 KB body  -> arrives CORRUPTED: every "&#61;" in a link comes back as a
                   raw "=" that has then been quoted-printable-decoded, so
                   "?id=87607238" lands with a bad byte where the "=8" was.
                   The same deletion runs through the whole body including the
                   18 KB <style> block, which is what destroyed the layout.
    85 KB body  -> never delivered at all. Accepted by the API, never arrived.

So above roughly 80 KB the large-message path stops encoding "=" correctly and
starts dropping messages. GMAIL_CLIP_BYTES drops to 55,000 so overflow.py sheds
the difference into the PDF, which loses nothing. Both audiences now go out at
~52 KB, on the path that demonstrably works.

This is a floor, not a fix. The real answer is to stop shipping a
stylesheet-dependent 100 KB body -- inline the critical CSS, or send a light
email with the report attached. Do not raise this number without re-probing.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Vnt6SJK5LgkQPDc7X6ThUW
